at's actually something we've thought  a lot about it a flowchart or almost  like a decision to reformat that's  easier to navigate than a fact sheet so  yes we agree with you that that would be  really helpful we haven't made it happen  yet but that is definitely something  that's on our radar so if you have any  specific ideas for components that would  go into that and that you'd like to see  please feel free to pass them along  because that is something that we are  kind of on the back burner at the moment  vote I would love to see that happen we  get questions regarding salt water over  wash from Hurricane sandy there were  home heating oil spills from flooding so  contamination do the weather related  episodes some so we get question of  whether it's safe to plant vegetables on  their property now three years later  yeah we had gotten a lot of questions  following Hurricane sandy as you might  imagine because we work closely with a  number of gardeners and organizations in  New York City I don't have the specific  resources in front of me but we do  actually have some information written  up that I would be happy to share if  that would be helpful the gist of it  basically was that given time the soil  should be able to recover from the salt  and the thing to be concerned about  would be if there were noticeable signs  of something like an oil spill so no an  oily residue in the soil or a chemical  smell either petroleum smell or evidence  that some sort of other chemical  had made its way into the garden as a  result of the flooding depending on the  specifics of what was there I could  consult with my team to see about what  we're looking at three years later so  kind of just the basic oh we've had  we've had a lot of salts it's very  likely that systems are in good shape by  now if there was a more specific  contaminant spill that could possibly be  something that could be addressed  through testing and bringing clean  materials in figuring out exactly what's  going on if that hasn't happened yet but  I'd be happy to talk further and share  what we had shared with gardeners at the  time getting questions about soil  pesticide contamination and residue any  information you have related to helping  ease people's concerns about that sure  so pesticides is a really broad category  of potential issues one thing that's  been on our radar a lot is the past use  of lead arsenate on former orchard plans  especially and also some different types  of growing areas but yeah we've actually  done a lot of research here at the  dillman Hill student farm on campus  which is a which was an orchard site in  the past and was heavily impacted by LED  arsenate contaminations so highly  elevated levels of both lead and arsenic  which bad news for students far but has  actually turned into a really great  piece for the opportunity and the staff  and students at the farm have worked  with us and others over a number of  years now to be sure that they're  growing food in areas that are not  impacted by the contamination and we've  been able to learn a lot for our own  projects by taking advantage of this  site that was impacted by this  historical contamination so that's one  specific example of one type of past  pesticide use that has had lasting  repercussions and that's kind of a big  one because there's a lot of former  orchard land that's now being used for  gardening and other purposes and farming  we kind of think about identifying  contaminant problems as detective work  so Paul thinking about  cific alee what may have happened on a  set on a site sometimes we know exactly  hey this gardener or this landscaper  came in and applied this particular  product what does this mean and in that  case you can look up the material safety  data sheets you can possibly test for a  particular chemical in other cases it's  more of a vague understanding that may  beat something viewed in the past in  general we recommend testing for metals  as a starting point if there are  possible concerns about contamination so  if there were lead arsenate used that  would show up in the form of lead and  arsenic testing for organic pesticides  for example gets very expensive very  quickly so we don't generally recommend  that people test for other specific  contaminants beyond metals unless they  really have cause to think that it's an  issue with their site in some specific  question that they think the testing can  help with otherwise what might be a  better alternative is to put the money  that they would have spent on testing  into getting clean materials and  possibly just covering up the known or  suspected problem at a given site that's  not always possible but it's also not  always possible for someone to spend  hundreds or thousands of dollars on soil  testing particularly in a residential  situation so it's kind of a trade-off of  considering what we know about this site  what's likely to be a problem what  resources are available including money  and then figuring out the balance of  okay it should be focused on testing to  learn more which is that deal or do we  just put that money into implementing  best practices that can reduce contacts  with the contaminants even if they may  be buried under kind of the working  surface a  so that's really where this 10 best  practices for healthy gardening emerged  out of that thought of like how can we  be make best use of our resources  definitely and in a cost-effective way  site remediation meaning actually  removing contaminated soil is very  rarely an option for a lot of the  gardener's that we work with because if  you think about it you've you know  you've got to have the money to bring in  the equipment to get the soil out of  their son got to go to a landfill  especially in New York City logistics of  moving things and transport could be  really daunting so what often makes more  sense is to instead cover up the  contamination and just start fresh so if  you're growing food that often means  raised beds or you know putting some  type of barrier down and then bringing  in clean material you know a framed bed  can work a mound of clean soil over a  surface that's you know that's blocking  off the contaminated material can also  work so it's it's difficult to give very  specific recommendations because as I  said before a lot of sites can benefit  from flexibility of finding what works  for them but these are kind of the basic  principles that we want people to keep  in mind if the pH is very different than  the normal surrounding soil should be  eat or you know are there any like hints  that there might be heavy metal  contamination it's like any red flag  testing that you can do this  less-expensive not really is the tricky  part you know for example we can see  plants thriving and soils that are very  high and lead because that is not toxic  to plants the way it is to people if  certain metals are our phytotoxic but  those don't tend to be the same ones  that are a concern for human health the  what we find most helpful is thinking  about the most common sources of  contaminants so for lead lead paint is  really a big one in both urban and rural  areas so if the if a garden or soil of  any kinds is adjacent to an older  painted structure that would have had  blood pain at some time in the past it's  very likely that there may be elevated  levels of lead  closest to that structure we tend to see  the highest levels right by the building  and then it decreases from there  sometimes there was an old out building  on the site you know an old garage or an  old shed or something that would have  been painted and that building make it  taken down and the current owner may not  even remember that that had been there  or know that it had been there but if we  were to test the whole site we may see  kind of this legacy of that past pain  LED from leaded gasoline was also a big  impact on lead levels so places closer  to busier roadways can be impacted by  LED specifically and then also the lead  arsenate from pesticide use and the  possible sources are a little different  for each of the different metals in our  fact sheets you can read more about  specific contaminants and where they may  have come from but it's you know kind of  so for led you can't see it or smell it  for organic contaminants you know if you  smell something that doesn't seem quite  right if you see an oily residue that  might be evidence that something was  going on if you know that burning had  happened at a site known old burn barrel  could give you a clue that that might be  an area to pay attention to but other  than these types of clues and trying to  parse out the site history unfortunately  there aren't there generally aren't easy  ways to look at the soil or look at the  plants and know that there's a problem  unless you see a pool of oil or smell  something but that's kind of an extreme  case  we're always looking to hear from people  the more we hear from people the more we  can make a strong case that we should  exist and that our program should be  here so you know where we're happy to to  hear in Currys directly from you please  pass along questions from people you  work with in your counties that you know  that we may be able to help answer you  know please please make use of us so  that we can continue to exist and be  here thank you everyone  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu


